---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="About"
  description="Senior Product Designer for fraud, payments, and trust decision systems. Formerly Kount, an Equifax company."
>
  <header class="hero shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 class="display hero__title">
      I design the surfaces people use when a model, a policy, and a person have to agree.
    </h1>
    <p class="hero__sub">
      Fraud, payments, and trust decision systems. Ten years in product design, most recently leading work on Kount, Equifax's fraud platform, on interfaces that reviewed more than a million transactions a day.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose shell">
    <h2>The work</h2>
    <p>
      Risk products fail in a specific way. The model can be right and the reviewer still cannot defend the decision. The policy can be sound and still unpublished because the editor is a production risk. The network can set a floor and the bank reviewing the payment still cannot tell whose rule fired.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is the work I want. I led product design on payments fraud, chargebacks, account protection, authorized payment protection, and the Policy Management surface on Kount 360, including the original information architecture for that platform. I sit with data science on what a score is allowed to reveal, and with engineering on what a configuration surface is allowed to publish into live traffic.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also ship the interface in code when that is the fastest way to prove the argument. The trust score explainer exists as a React and Storybook kit, not only as a writeup.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this work</h2>
    <p>
      I came to design sideways. Four and a half years in a biochemistry lab, cognitive science with an HCI focus at UC San Diego, then a Master of Engineering in Product Design and Development Management at Northwestern.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lab is the part that still shows up. Bench science teaches you that the hard part of a problem is usually the measurement, not the answer, and that a result you cannot explain is not a result yet. Fraud tooling is the same job in a different building.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>
      In these products the interface is downstream of a model, a policy, and a regulator. None of those are negotiable from Figma. I spend longer than most designers on what the system actually does, then design the smallest surface that makes origin, authority, and next action legible.
    </p>
    <p>
      I speak Spanish. On authorized payment protection that put me in the stakeholder room with a Latin American payment network instead of reading the meeting afterward.
    </p>

    <h2>Now</h2>
    <p>
      Seattle, working remotely. Open to senior and staff product design roles, and to hybrid design-engineer seats, on risk, payments, and trust products. This site is Astro. The component kit is React, TypeScript, and Storybook.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero { padding-block: var(--space-3xl) var(--space-2xl); }
  .hero__title { font-size: var(--type-h1); margin-block-start: var(--space-lg); max-width: 28ch; }
  .hero__sub {
    font-size: var(--type-body-lg);
    line-height: 1.58;
    color: var(--ink-secondary);
    max-width: 58ch;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-lg);
  }
  .prose { padding-block-end: var(--space-3xl); }
</style>
